{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_bxylqlu7vgw2vzv7uwogtmho7lbytohxykclciwnn2bnufyp3sva","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 6 integration tests pass — the Rust binary produces byte-identical stdout to the C reference binary across multiple seeds (0, 1, 42, UINT_MAX) and matches exit codes for invalid seed inputs.\n\nSummary:\n\n- **Cargo.toml `[features]`**: none — there is exactly one build configuration to verify, so step 9's per-feature loop has a single iteration.\n- **C build system**: `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` declares only `add_executable(driver src/main.c)` — no shared library target, no preprocessor `#define` toggles. Built it with `cmake -DCMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=ON && cmake --build .` and got `c_src/build/driver`.\n- **Rust crate**: `[[bin]]`-only, no `cdylib`. The C and Rust both expose only `main()`; there is no public C API, no headers (the C source has no `#include` of project headers), and no `#[no_mangle]` exports. Consequently `nm -D` on a Rust `.so` and a C `.so` is not applicable here — neither side produces shared libraries.\n- **Adjusted approach**: since `c_src/` must not be modified and there is no library boundary to cross, the equivalence test compares the two executables end-to-end. `tests/binary_equivalence.rs` runs both binaries with the same seed and asserts byte-identical stdout and matching exit codes.\n- **Added**: `libloading = \"0.8\"` to `[dev-dependencies]` per the instructions, even though the binary-only design means the tests didn't end up needing it.\n- **Result**: `cargo test --release --test binary_equivalence` — 6/6 passed in 537s. Seeds 0, 1, 42, and 4294967295 all produced identical stdout between the C and Rust binaries; invalid seeds (`12abc`, `9999999999`) produced matching nonzero exit codes."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14594,"cache_read_input_toke